1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,526
In the last few episodes we kept writing assembly,

2
00:00:09,526 --> 00:00:15,048
but what the hardware really executes is a series of thirty-two bit binary numbers.

3
00:00:15,048 --> 00:00:22,866
Instructions are themselves data, stored in memory just like ordinary data, and that is the stored program idea.

4
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:26,805
The PC holds the address of the current instruction in memory.

5
00:00:26,805 --> 00:00:32,466
So how does an assembly instruction actually turn into thirty-two bits?

6
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:37,030
The thirty-two bits are cut into pieces, and each piece is called a field.

7
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:42,088
Instructions like add and sub, whose operands are all registers, use the R format.

8
00:00:42,088 --> 00:00:47,393
The fields rd, rs1, and rs2 take five bits each, since two to the fifth is thirty-two,

9
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:50,291
just enough to number the thirty-two registers.

10
00:00:50,291 --> 00:00:55,349
The opcode takes the lowest seven bits and says which kind of instruction this is,

11
00:00:55,349 --> 00:00:59,166
while funct3 and funct7 narrow down the exact operation.

12
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:01,873
Let's encode add x18, x19, x10.

13
00:01:01,873 --> 00:01:08,160
Here rd is eighteen, which is one zero zero one zero in five-bit binary.

14
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:17,433
Then rs1 is nineteen, which is one zero zero one one, and rs2 is ten, which is zero one zero one zero.

15
00:01:17,433 --> 00:01:26,679
For add, funct3 and funct7 are both zero, and the opcode for R-type arithmetic is zero one one zero zero one one.

16
00:01:26,679 --> 00:01:34,833
Putting every four bits together as one hexadecimal digit, the whole instruction is 0x00A98933.

17
00:01:34,833 --> 00:01:35,901
What about sub?

18
00:01:35,901 --> 00:01:40,032
Only funct7 changes, to zero one zero zero zero zero zero.

19
00:01:40,032 --> 00:01:46,733
When the hardware sees that one in bit thirty, it makes the adder subtract.

20
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:55,433
All the R-type operations share one opcode, and are told apart by the combination of funct3 and funct7.

21
00:01:55,433 --> 00:01:59,665
The addi instruction has only two registers, plus a constant.

22
00:01:59,665 --> 00:02:01,539
Where does the constant go?

23
00:02:01,539 --> 00:02:08,616
The I format merges the funct7 and rs2 positions from the R format into a single twelve-bit immediate,

24
00:02:08,616 --> 00:02:12,866
and leaves all the other fields exactly where they were.

25
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:15,572
That answers the question from episode two.

26
00:02:15,572 --> 00:02:18,215
The immediate gets only these twelve bits,

27
00:02:18,215 --> 00:02:25,200
and the range of a twelve-bit two's complement number is exactly minus two thousand forty-eight to two thousand

28
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,333
forty-seven.

29
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:30,439
Let's encode addi x15, x1, minus fifty.

30
00:02:30,439 --> 00:02:39,042
Minus fifty as a twelve-bit two's complement number is one one one one, one one zero zero, one one one zero.

31
00:02:39,042 --> 00:02:46,768
When it executes, the hardware sign-extends those twelve bits to thirty-two and adds them to rs1.

32
00:02:46,768 --> 00:02:51,066
The encoding comes out as 0xFCE08793.

33
00:02:51,066 --> 00:02:54,303
Load instructions are also I format.

34
00:02:54,303 --> 00:03:02,766
In lw x14, 8(x2), the offset eight is the immediate, and the base register x2 goes in rs1.

35
00:03:02,766 --> 00:03:11,741
All the loads share one opcode, and funct3 tells apart the width and the signedness, for lb, lh, lw, lbu, and lhu.

36
00:03:11,741 --> 00:03:20,007
The shift instructions slli, srli, and srai are I format too, but the shift amount is at most thirty-one,

37
00:03:20,007 --> 00:03:25,466
so only the low five bits of the immediate are used.

38
00:03:25,466 --> 00:03:30,959
A store has two source registers, the data and the base, and no destination register,

39
00:03:30,959 --> 00:03:33,480
but it still needs a twelve-bit offset.

40
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:36,711
The S format splits the immediate into two pieces.

41
00:03:36,711 --> 00:03:43,466
The upper seven bits go in the old place of funct7, and the lower five bits go where rd used to be.

42
00:03:43,466 --> 00:03:45,687
Let's encode sw x14, 8(x2).

43
00:03:45,687 --> 00:03:53,006
The offset eight in twelve-bit binary is seven zeros followed by zero one zero zero zero.

44
00:03:53,006 --> 00:04:00,766
So the upper seven bits are all zero, and the lower five bits are zero one zero zero zero.

45
00:04:00,766 --> 00:04:06,660
The data in x14 goes into rs2 and the base x2 goes into rs1.

46
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:16,189
Then funct3 is zero one zero for a whole word, and the opcode is zero one zero zero zero one one.

47
00:04:16,189 --> 00:04:22,100
Put together, this sw is 0x00E12423.

48
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,990
Now let's stack the three formats on top of each other.

49
00:04:25,990 --> 00:04:28,748
Why is the S format split so awkwardly?

50
00:04:28,748 --> 00:04:34,900
The answer is that whenever a format uses rs1 and rs2, they sit in the same place.

51
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:37,361
The same goes for funct3 and the opcode.

52
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:44,562
So the hardware can read the registers straight from fixed positions, without first working out the instruction type.

53
00:04:44,562 --> 00:04:50,902
Reading registers and decoding can happen at the same time, which makes the circuit simpler and faster,

54
00:04:50,902 --> 00:04:54,900
and is a really neat touch in the design of RISC-V.

